<p><span class="j">My proposal for the "How To", which you also left to me.</span> One short tab that answers "what is this thing and where do I act", so the page explains itself in six months when neither of us remembers.</p>
<h4>The loop, in one line each</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Observe.</b> A free script reads the earnings calendar, your live holdings, the cohorts and your mailbox, every 30 minutes. No tokens</li>
<li><b>Orient.</b> It works out each company's state, whether its window is open, whether a reply or a chaser is owed, and how warm the email should be, from whether you own it, have owned it, or have written to them</li>
<li><b>Decide.</b> It derives the ask from your rules: market capitalisation, ownership, and the type you are realistically offered. Nothing waits for you to approve a list</li>
<li><b>Act.</b> Superhuman writes each draft from a short instruction carrying those facts. The drafts land in the Company Roadshows and Meetings tab of your daily Executive Assistant page</li>
<li><b>You.</b> Press send. That is the only step that is yours</li>
<li><b>Then.</b> Replies, slots and bookings run through your existing daily email run and the calendar rules, as they do today</li>
</ul>
<h4>Where each thing reaches you</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>This page:</b> status only. It never asks you anything</li>
<li><b>Your daily Executive Assistant page:</b> every draft to send, every question, every choice, in its own tab</li>
<li><b>The requests page:</b> a new box for this workstream, so "email Instalco's investor relations team" works the way it worked for Vodafone on Sunday</li>
<li><b>The Daily Digest:</b> only when something has broken</li>
</ul>
<h4>What the states mean</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Nothing due:</b> the company's results are more than two weeks away</li>
<li><b>Window open:</b> from about two weeks before results to about one week after, which is when investor relations teams allocate roadshow slots</li>
<li><b>Drafted:</b> written and waiting on your send</li>
<li><b>Sent:</b> out, and the chaser clock runs from this date, never from the date it was drafted</li>
<li><b>Chased once, chased twice:</b> then it stops and shows as no reply</li>
<li><b>Booked, held:</b> in the diary, then done</li>
<li><b>Window missed:</b> results passed with no approach, so the ask switches from a roadshow place to a standalone call</li>
</ul>
<h4>The guideline this is sized against, in your words</h4>
<p><i>"6-10 a week in 'earnings season' is acceptable/normal. And 1/4 to 1/3 that amount normally. These are v. rough guidelines, not rules."</i> The page shows the week's count against that, as information and never as a limit it enforces.</p>
